coder › Done. Here's what I changed and why.

## Changes

**`src/range.ts`**
- The comparison is now strict: `aStart < bEnd && bStart < aEnd`. Since the end is exclusive, two ranges that merely touch (one ends exactly when the other starts) no longer count as overlapping.
- Two ranges starting at the same instant still overlap, because their shared instant falls before each range's exclusive end.
- A zero-length range (`start === end`) contains no moment, so it returns `false` early — it overlaps nothing, not even itself. This also avoids the strict comparison producing a false positive for two identical zero-length points.
- Parsing goes through a `parse` helper that throws `TypeError` with the offending value in the message when `Date.parse` yields `NaN` (e.g. `"cannot parse range value: not-a-date"`), instead of silently returning `false`.
- A range whose end is before its start throws `RangeError`, also naming the range.

**`src/range.test.ts`**
Added tests for: touching ranges (both argument orders), same-instant starts, zero-length ranges (as either argument and against itself), unparseable start → `TypeError` naming the value, unparseable end → `TypeError` naming the value, and end-before-start → `RangeError`.

